WEBVTT
TITLE I/O Interface, Interrupts and DMA — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
A disk or a keyboard is not a memory. It cannot be asked for the contents of an address.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
So the device gets a translator: the interface, or controller. It presents three registers to the bus.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
Every I/O method in the syllabus is an answer to one question: who reads and writes these three registers?

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
One word in, one word out: a keyboard gives a byte per keystroke, a disk gives a block. The interface absorbs that mismatch.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
There are two ways to name a register: put it in the memory address space, or give it a space of its own.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
Memory-mapped costs addresses. In a 16-bit space of 65536 addresses, 256 port addresses leave 65280 for memory.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
Isolated costs instructions and one extra control line, not addresses.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
Memory-mapped lets any instruction touch a device; isolated keeps devices out of the memory space. Both are used in real machines.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
Programmed I/O, or polling: the CPU reads the status register in a loop until it says ready.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
The cost of one byte is the whole time that byte took, because the CPU was busy the whole time.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
A keyboard polled every 10 ms at 100 microseconds a poll: 100 polls a second cost 10 ms a second.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
Polling is cheap in hardware and expensive in CPU. That trade is the whole reason interrupts exist.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
Interrupt-driven I/O turns the loop inside out: the CPU works on something else, and the device raises a line when it is ready.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
The order is forced. First finish the current instruction, because an interrupt is honoured only at a boundary.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
Then push the process status, because the program counter is part of it. Save before you jump.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
Then load the new program counter from the vector, run the routine, and pop the status to resume.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
Two different jobs get confused here: naming the source, and ranking the sources.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
Vectored: the device supplies the address itself. Non-vectored: every device shares one address, and software must ask around.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
Daisy chain is hardware ranking: the acknowledge signal passes device to device and stops at the first one asking.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
Maskable interrupts can be switched off by software. A non-maskable interrupt cannot, and it is reserved for emergencies.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
DMA hands the copying to a controller with three registers: address, count, and control.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
The handshake is two wires: the controller requests the bus, and the CPU grants it.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
Only one bus master at a time. The CPU and the controller are never both driving the bus.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
The CPU pays at two moments only: the setup before, and the completion interrupt after.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
Burst mode: the controller takes the bus once and keeps it for the whole block.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
Cycle stealing: one word per grant, then the bus goes back. The CPU loses one cycle at a time.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
Transparent mode: the controller waits for cycles the CPU is not using, so the CPU never stops.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
One picture answers all three: how long the bus is held, and how much of that time the CPU loses.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
Cycle stealing gives one formula: throughput is clock rate, times the fraction of cycles stolen, times the bits per stolen cycle.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
For interrupts, the fraction of CPU time is the cost per interrupt divided by the interval between interrupts.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
Interrupt against programmed: 10 KB a second, 4 microseconds of overhead, so 100 microseconds of waiting against 4.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
Cycle stealing on a disk: a 4-byte word every 152.6 nanoseconds against a 40-nanosecond memory cycle.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
A register of n bits counts to 2 to the n. A 16-bit count register therefore covers 65536 bytes in one transfer.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
29154 kilobytes against 64 kilobytes is 455.5, so the transfer needs 456 bus acquisitions, not 455.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
A software copy loop costs a few instructions per byte. Count them, then count the DMA path, then divide.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
Both of these are just bookkeeping. The reasoning is in the count, and the answer is one division.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
Step one: name the registers. Step two: name who copies. Everything else follows.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
Step three: write down the count in the units the question uses. Step four: divide once, and check the units.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
The five traps: identification against ranking, save before jump, one bus master, rounding down, and a fraction above one.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
One topic, one question: who reads and writes the three registers, and how often.

